  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Lao and Thai",
      "body": "<p>The Lao and Thai scripts grew from the same parent in the 13th and 14th centuries, and the languages are close enough that many speakers understand each other. Lao letters are rounder and simpler, and Lao spelling was reformed to be written more as it sounds: it has 27 consonants where Thai has 44, because the extra Thai letters for Pali and Sanskrit words were dropped.</p>\n<p>Like Thai, Lao is written without spaces between words, with vowels around the consonant and tone marks above it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Classes and tones",
      "body": "<p>Vientiane Lao has six tones. As in Thai, the spelling shows the tone through three things together: the class of the first consonant (middle, high or low), the tone mark (<span lang=\"lo\">່</span> mai ek or <span lang=\"lo\">້</span> mai tho, and two rarer ones), and whether the syllable is live (ending in a long vowel, m, n, ng, w or y) or dead (a short vowel, or p, t, k).</p>\n<p>Because the tones differ from region to region, Lao teachers usually give the rules for the capital, Vientiane. The sounds in this app are spoken without tones, so listen to a native speaker for the melody.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "ຫ, the silent helper",
      "body": "<p>Low-class letters like ນ, ມ, ລ and ວ have no high-class twin. To spell a word with their sound and a high-class tone, Lao writes a silent ຫ in front: <span lang=\"lo\">ຫວານ</span> (wan, sweet). Two of these pairs are so common that they are written as one letter: <span lang=\"lo\">ໜ</span> for ຫນ and <span lang=\"lo\">ໝ</span> for ຫມ.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
